  "text_md": "REDUCED SUPERSCHEMES AND THE COMBINATORICS OF TORIC SUPERVARIETIES 21\n\n= Cθ2\n\nVρ\n\n2\n\nVρ\n\n0\n\n= 0\n\nVρ\n\n1\n\n= Cθ1\n\nFigure 3. The decorated fan of an action of Q(1)2 on P2|2\n\nExample 4.28. Let us use Proposition 4.26 and Corollary 4.27 to classify toric supervarieties with supertorus Q(1)n and underlying variety Pn ∼= XΣ for the complete fan whose rays are ρi = R+xi for i = 1,...,n and ρ0 = R+(−x1 − ... − xn).\n\n# ] ⊆ Cxi. Hence Vρ\n\nFor i = 1,...,n, the ray ρi must be decorated by a subspace Vρ\n\n# such that [Vρ\n\n# ,Vρ\n\ni\n\ni\n\ni\n\n= C(θ1 ± θ2 ± ... ± θn) for some 2n−1 choices of ±.\n\n# = Cθi or 0. Likewise, unless Vρ\n\n# = 0, we have Vρ\n\n0\n\n0\n\ni\n\nIt is straightforward to verify that condition (b) of the definition of a large-orbit decorated fan holds regardless of which subspaces are chosen. We therefore obtain a collection of 2n(1+2n−1) toric supervarieties which are not equivariantly isomorphic. Many, however, are isomorphic via toric morphisms (to be defined in the following section).\n\nNotice that if all but one of these decorations is nonzero, then the decorated fan describes a supervariety isomorphic to projective superspace Pn|n. For instance, if Vρ\n\n# = Cθi and Vρ\n\ni\n\n= 0, then the coordinate superalgebras of the affine charts can be written as\n\n0\n\nC[t1,...,tn,t1ξ1,...,tnξn] and\n\nC[t−i 1t1,...,t−i 1,...,t−i 1tnξ1,...,ξi,...,t−i 1tnξn]. Figure 3 depicts the corresponding decorated fan for n = 2,\n\nIf instead we change the decoration of ρ0 to θ1 + ... + θn, then the resulting affine charts have coordinate superalgebras\n\nC[t1,...,tn,t1ξ1,...,tnξn] and\n\nC[t−i 1t1(1 + ξiξ1),...,t−i 1,...,t−i 1tn(1 + ξiξn),t−i 1t1(ξi − ξ1),...,t−i 1ξi,...,t−i 1tn(ξi − ξn)], so the supervariety is decidedly not isomorphic to projective superspace. Figure 4(B) depicts corresponding decorated fan for n = 1.\n\nIn general, when T = Q(1)n, there are finitely many possible decorations for each ray. Namely, for ρ = R+(a1x1 + ... + anxn), there are 2d−1 many possible “square root subspaces” C(√a1θ1 ±...±\n\n√anθn), where d is the number of indices i = 1,...,n for which ai ̸= 0.\n\nThe prior example admitted no issues of compatibility between different rays of the same cone. This is not ordinarily the case; if ρ1 = R+(a1x1 + ... + anxn) and ρ2 = R+(b1x1 + ... + bnxn), compatibility is less common if the linear matroid on the n vectors\n\n- a1\n- b1\n\n\n,...,\n\nan bn\n\n",
